<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌫️ LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/fog/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t04z-fog-anl-g5" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Fog index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>0 No observed fog</b> sage green &rarr; <b>20 Fog risk</b> yellow-green (risk owns 0-35: thermodynamic base x calm-air factor) &rarr; <b>40 Active fog</b> gold (VIS 4797 m / 2.98 mi) &rarr; <b>60 Active fog</b> brick red (VIS 3486 m / 2.17 mi) &rarr; <b>80 Active fog</b> violet (VIS 1842 m / 1.14 mi) &rarr; <b>100 Active fog</b> deep purple (VIS 0 m). Active fog by corroborated density owns 35-100 (gate: VIS below 5000 m with RH at/above 90%); dry-air speckles can never paint active fog. Same index always shows the same color — not a copy of the visibility layer.</p><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (derived HRRR analysis), hourly cycles</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km analysis, hourly cycles: TMP+DPT+RH (2 m) + UGRD/VGRD (10 m) + VIS surface (derived fog-risk index with active-fog visibility gate)<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE FOG RISK/ACTIVE FOG</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/fog/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261008-t04z-fog-anl-g5" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Fog index 0-100 (DERIVED from HRRR analysis, fixed absolute scale): <b>0 No observed fog</b> sage green &rarr; <b>20 Fog risk</b> yellow-green (risk owns 0-35: thermodynamic base x calm-air factor) &rarr; <b>40 Active fog</b> gold (VIS 4797 m / 2.98 mi) &rarr; <b>60 Active fog</b> brick red (VIS 3486 m / 2.17 mi) &rarr; <b>80 Active fog</b> violet (VIS 1842 m / 1.14 mi) &rarr; <b>100 Active fog</b> deep purple (VIS 0 m). Active fog by corroborated density owns 35-100 (gate: VIS below 5000 m with RH at/above 90%); dry-air speckles can never paint active fog. Same index always shows the same color — not a copy of the visibility layer.</p><p>A derived fog-risk index from operational HRRR analysis: low greens mark clear air, yellows and oranges mark elevated fog risk from saturated calm air, and reds into deep purple mark active fog confirmed by collapsed visibility. Built for Great Lakes navigation, shoreline conditions, aviation awareness, and nighttime environmental monitoring. Derivation: v2: risk r = rh_factor*spread_factor*calm_factor in [0,1] (rh_factor=clip((RH-70)/30,0,1); spread_factor=clip((3-S)/3,0,1), S=TMP-DPT C deg; calm_factor=clip((6-wspd)/6,0.35,1)) -> risk zone 35*r. Corroborated density d = clip((5000-VIS)/5000,0,1)^0.8 only where VIS<5000m & RH>=90 -> fog zone 35+65*d. Index = max of the two (continuous at VIS=5000m). Risk owns 0-35, active fog by density owns 35-100. Distinct from raw visibility; dry-air speckles can never paint active fog.</p><p>Fog science (boundary-layer meteorology and aerosol microphysics) studies saturation, radiative cooling, and droplet nucleation that reduce visibility below one kilometer. Radiation fog forms over land and calm water on clear nights; advection and steam fog form where air-water temperature contrasts drive lake-effect events. The index separates fog likelihood from raw visibility so developing risk reads before, and active fog reads during, an event.</p><p><b>Units:</b> fog index 0-100 (display; derived, see derivation)<br><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km analysis, hourly cycles: TMP+DPT+RH (2 m) + UGRD/VGRD (10 m) + VIS surface (derived fog-risk index with active-fog visibility gate)<br><b>Update:</b> Checked hourly; republishes only on a newer HRRR cycle.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p>]]></description>
